0 (S)\". Wait, the Part line says \"DM, BMxxx\" — generic. Tables give exact. I'll use the exact table labels for model IDs and put generic in type_designations.\n\nLet me structure:\n- components[0]:\n  - role: \"measurement_transducer\"\n  - producer: \"Silea Liquid Transfer S.r.l.\"\n  - type_designations: [\"DM\", \"BMxxx\"] (as stated in Part line) — and maybe add \"documentation_folder\": \"TC12118-1\".\n  - characteristics:\n    - type_level:\n      - maximum_pressure: {value: 1034, unit_symbol: \"kPa\"}\n      - environmental_class: {value: \"M3\"}\n      - ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n      - product_temperature_range: {value: {min: -5, max: 35}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n      - intended_measurement: \"Hydrocarbon based oils and fuels with maximum viscosity of 20 mPa·s at 20 °C\" — structured? Could be {value: \"Hydrocarbon based oils and fuels\", ...} plus max viscosity 20 mPa·s at 20°C. I'll do:\n        - intended_fluid: {value: \"Hydrocarbon based oils and fuels\"}\n        - maximum_viscosity: {value: 20, unit_symbol: \"mPa·s\", with note \"at 20 °C\"}? The structured form allows footnote_markers only. I could set value: 20, unit_symbol: \"mPa·s\" and add reference_temperature: 20°C. To keep simple: \"maximum_viscosity\": {value: 20, unit_symbol: \"mPa·s\"} and \"viscosity_reference_temperature\": {value: 20, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}. Good.\n      - impulse_encoder_or_pulser: {value: \"Eltomatic A/S Impulse Encoder, type 01-09\"} — this is a sub-component! Should it be a components[] entry? It's named as part: \"Impulse encoder or pulser: Eltomatic A/S Impulse Encoder, type 01-09\". I could add a nested component entry with role \"impulse_encoder\" attached to transducer, or a top-level component. The schema components[] is flat. I'll include it in the transducer's characteristics as type_level value string, and maybe also as separate component? I'll just put in characteristics as \"impulse_encoder_or_pulser\": {value: \"Eltomatic A/S Impulse Encoder, type 01-09\"}. Fine.\n    - model_level: The meter tables 3 & 4 give per-model: accuracy_class, flange_size, cyclic_volume, qmin, qmax, mmq.\n      - models: BM250 (S), BM950 (S), BM450 (D), BM550 (D), BM350 (D), BM650 (T), BM850 (T) [Table 3], DM (S) [Table 4].\n      \n      Wait — is Table 3 about the meter = the transducer-based meter? Yes, \"specific characteristics of the meter type BMxx\". And Table 4 \"meter type DM\". These are meters comprised of the transducer. So model_level attributes belong to this component (as meters).\n\n      Attributes per model:\n      - accuracy_class: 0.3 for all\n      - flange_size: \"2½\\\" (DN 65)\", \"3\\\" (DN80)\", \"4\\\" (DN100)\", \"6\\\" (DN150)\" — mixed inch + DN. Structured: value as string since composite. E.g. {value: \"2½\\\" (DN 65)\"}. Or split: flange_size_inch: 2.5 and dn: 65. 2½ = 2.5. I'll do value as string with DN, and maybe separate nominal_diameter. Simpler: flange_size with string value including both. But rule says numbers not strings when numeric... \"2½\\\" (DN 65)\" is composite → string OK.\n      - cyclic_volume: L: 2.27, 2.27, 4.54, 4.54, 4.54, 6.81, 6.81; DM: 5.75\n      - qmin: L/min: 115, 115, 200, 220, 125, 300, 200; DM: 200\n      - qmax: L/min: 1140, 1500, 2050, 2280, 2800, 3000, 4250; DM: 2500\n      - mmq: L: 100, 200, 200, 500, 500, 500, 500; DM: 200\n\n**Component 2: Measurement sensor (Emerson)**\n- producer: \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\"\n- type_designations: Part line: \"DMFxxxy, DS600\" — but tables say CMFxxxy. I'll use [\"CMFxxxy\", \"DS600\"] and maybe include all specific models. Given the OCR discrepancy, I might note... no notes. I'll go with CMFxxxy (consistent with tables listing CMF010 etc.). Hmm, should I preserve \"DMFxxxy\" somewhere? The tables strongly indicate CMF (Micro Motion). The heading of Table 5: \"measurement sensor type CMFxxxy, DS600\". I'll use CMFxxxy.\n- documentation_folder: TC7056-13\n- characteristics.type_level (Table 5):\n  - maximum_pressure: {value: 413, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n  - accuracy_class: {value: \"0.3 and 0.5\"}? Hmm — accuracy class has two values. It's class 0.3 or 0.5 depending on configuration. Could do value: [0.3, 0.5]? The structured form doesn't include lists. I could do string \"0.3 / 0.5\". Or handle in model_level with axis accuracy class... Actually in Table 6, accuracy class is a row dimension: each sensor model has two rows (0.3 and 0.5) with different Qmin. So accuracy class is a config axis! Qmin varies by (model × accuracy class). \n  \n  For type_level: accuracy_class: {value: \"0.3 / 0.5\"} — hmm. Let me just use a list? Rules say lists of values for Emax → model matrix. Here it's two possible classes. I'll represent as config axis in model_level: for each model, two config conditions: \"accuracy class 0.3\" and \"accuracy class 0.5\".\n  \n  - environmental_class: {value: \"M3 / E3\"}\n  - ambient_temperature_range: {min: -40, max: 55}, °C\n  - product_temperature_range: {min: -40, max: 250}, °C\n  - intended_fluid: \"Oil and oil product\"\n  - maximum_viscosity: 1242 mPa·s\n\n- Table 6 matrix: models: CMF010, CMF025, CMF050, CMF100, CMF200, CMF300, CMF350, CMF400 (A), CMF400 (B), CMF400 (C), DS600 S, CMFHC2, CMFHC3, CMFHC4.\n\n  Attributes: accuracy_class (axis), qmin (kg/min), qmax (kg/min), mmq (kg), maximum_pressure (bar(g)).\n\n  The pressure depends on material suffix y with footnote markers. E.g., CMF010: 125 (3) → 125 bar(g) when y∈{A,L,M}; 225 (4) → y∈{B,C,E,H}; 413 (2) → y=P.\n\n  This is a config-level matrix: attribute maximum_pressure, axis \"material suffix y\", conditions \"y = H, L or M\" etc. Per the schema, config_level: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value}]}.\n\n  And Qmin depends on (model, accuracy_class) and for some models also material (CMF010: 0.033 (1) / 0.067 (2); CMF350: 226 (1; 2), 453 (6)).\n\n  How to represent Qmin: model_level with values per model, but each model has two values (class 0.3, 0.5). Could do model entries \"CMF010 (class 0.3)\" and \"CMF010 (class 0.5)\". Or config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\" and conditions \"CMF010, class 0.3\"... \n\n  I think the cleanest: model_level entries with model IDs including accuracy class, e.g. {\"model\": \"CMF010 (class 0.3)\", \"value\": 0.033, ...}. Hmm, that doubles models to ~24 entries per attribute.\n\n  Alternatively use config_level for accuracy class: attribute qmin, axis \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"CMF010, class 0.3\", value: 0.033}, ...]. \n\n  I'll go with model_level using compound model IDs like \"CMF010 (0.3)\" — treating accuracy class as part of the variant identity, since each sensor is approved in class 0.3 or 0.5 configuration. Actually, since Table 6 lists accuracy class as a column (a dimension), config_level with axis \"accuracy class\" fits the schema better:\n\n  config_level[]: {\n    attribute: \"qmin\",\n    axis: \"accuracy_class\",\n    values: [{condition: \"CMF010 class 0.3\", value: {…0.033…}}, ...]\n  }\n\n  Hmm, but that loses model_level. The schema allows both model_level and config_level. Given the table is model × class, I'll use model_level with model IDs like \"CMF010\" and... no.\n\n  Let me just pick: **model_level with compound model IDs** including class, e.g. \"CMF010 (class 0.3)\". This keeps values[] entries {model, value} shape. And qmax, mmq, maximum_pressure don't vary by class → model_level with plain model IDs. But then models list has both \"CMF010\" and \"CMF010 (class 0.3)\" — inconsistent.\n\n  Alternative: models are \"CMF010\", \"CMF025\", ... and qmin is config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\" with conditions \"CMF010 accuracy class 0.3\" etc. And pressure is config_level with axis \"material suffix y\" conditions like \"y = A, L or M (3)\". \n\n  I'll do that:\n  - model_level: qmax (per model), mmq (per model).\n  - config_level: \n    - qmin, axis \"accuracy class (per model)\": conditions \"CMF010, class 0.3\" → 0.033 (footnote 1... wait footnote markers (1) and (2) refer to material for CMF010). Hmm CMF010 Qmin: class 0.3: 0.033 (1) / 0.067 (2) — two values by material within the same class! So CMF010 Qmin varies by both class and material. \n      - class 0.3: 0.033 when y∈{H,L,M} (1); 0.067 when y=P (2)\n      - class 0.5: 0.017 (1); 0.033 (2)\n    - CMF350: class 0.3: 226 (1; 2), 453 (6); class 0.5: 113 (1; 2), 226 (6).\n      - (1; 2): y = H, L, M (1) or P (2) → 226; (6): y ∈ {A,B,C,E} → 453.\n    - CMF400: qmin varies by (A)/(B)/(C) serial and class:\n      - (A): 680 both classes; (B): 680 / 340; (C): 1700 / 850.\n\n  OK this is genuinely multi-dimensional: model × class × material/serial. The best representation: matrix_tables preserves the raw table, and characteristics get a best-effort decomposition. I'll also make sure matrix_tables rows capture the cells including footnote markers.\n\n  Let me design matrix_tables[2] (Table 6):\n\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Specific characteristics of the measurement sensor type CMFxxxy, DS600\",\n    \"columns\": [\"type\", \"variant\", \"accuracy_class\", \"qmin\", \"qmax\", \"mmq\", \"maximum_pressure\"],\n    \"column_units\": [null, null, null, \"kg/min\", \"kg/min\", \"kg\", \"bar(g)\"],\n    \"rows\": [...],\n    \"footnotes\": [...]\n  }\n\n  Rows — flatten rowspan into one row per (type, class) with variant for CMF400:\n  1. {\"type\": \"CMF010\", \"variant\": \"y) = material suffix\", \"accuracy_class\": 0.3, \"qmin\": \"0.033 (1) / 0.067 (2)\", \"qmax\": 1.8, \"mmq\": 0.05, \"maximum_pressure\": \"125 (3); 225 (4); 413 (2)\"}\n  \n  Hmm — values as strings with footnote markers. But rule 2 says numbers not strings... For matrix_tables, the R137 example shows plain numbers in rows. For cells with multiple conditional values, string is the pragmatic option. Alternatively split into separate rows per condition. That explodes rows. I'll keep conditional multi-values as strings in matrix_tables rows, but decompose properly in characteristics.\n\n  Actually, let me reconsider: maybe I should represent footnote markers as footnote_markers arrays and the conditional values... The pressure \"125 (3); 225 (4); 413 (2)\" means: 125 when (3): y = A, L or M; 225 when (4): y = B, C, E or H; 413 when (2): y = P. I'll capture this properly in config_level:\n\n  config_level for maximum_pressure:\n  attribute: \"maximum_pressure\", axis: \"sensor material suffix y)\", values: [\n    {condition: \"CMF010, y = A, L or M\", value: 125, unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"},\n    {condition: \"CMF010, y = B, C, E or H\", value: 225, ...},\n    {condition: \"CMF010, y = P (high pressure version)\", value: 413, ...},\n    {condition: \"CMF025, y = A, L or M\", value: 103},\n    {condition: \"CMF025, y = B, C, E or H\", value: 190},\n    ...\n  ]\n\n  That's the full decomposition. It's verbose but correct. Let me enumerate:\n\n  Pressure values (footnote mapping):\n  - (3): y = A, L or M\n  - (4): y = B, C, E or H\n  - (2): y = P (high pressure version)\n  - (5): y = Y\n  - (6): y = A, B, C or E\n  - (1): y = H, L or M\n\n  - CMF010: 125 (3) → y∈{A,L,M}; 225 (4) → y∈{B,C,E,H}; 413 (2) → y=P\n  - CMF025: 103 (3) → y∈{A,L,M}; 190 (4) → y∈{B,C,E,H}\n  - CMF050: 103 (3); 185 (4)\n  - CMF100: 100 (3); 170 (4)\n  - CMF200: 108 (3); 190 (4)\n  - CMF300: 119 (3); 185 (4)\n  - CMF350: 102 (3) → y∈{A,L,M}; 115 (2; 4) → y=P or y∈{B,C,E,H}\n  - CMF400 (A/B/C share): 103 (3); 197 (4); 205 (2)\n  - DS600 S: 43 (no footnote)\n  - CMFHC2/HC3/HC4: 102 (3) → y∈{A,L,M}; 206 (5) → y=Y\n\n  Qmin (footnote mapping):\n  - (1): y = H, L or M\n  - (2): y = P\n  - (6): y = A, B, C or E\n\n  - CMF010: class 0.3: 0.033 (1) → y∈{H,L,M}; 0.067 (2) → y=P. class 0.5: 0.017 (1); 0.033 (2)\n  - CMF025: 0.3: 0.46; 0.5: 0.23\n  - CMF050: 0.3: 2.72; 0.5: 1.36\n  - CMF100: 0.3: 11.4; 0.5: 5.7\n  - CMF200: 0.3: 36; 0.5: 18\n  - CMF300: 0.3: 114; 0.5: 57\n  - CMF350: 0.3: 226 (1; 2) → y∈{H,L,M,P}; 453 (6) → y∈{A,B,C,E}. 0.5: 113 (1; 2); 226 (6)\n  - CMF400: varies by serial variant (A)/(B)/(C):\n    - (A): 0.3: 680; 0.5: 680\n    - (B): 0.3: 680; 0.5: 340\n    - (C): 0.3: 1700; 0.5: 850\n  - DS600 S: 0.3: 1140; 0.5: 570\n  - CMFHC2: 0.3: 1136; 0.5: 568\n  - CMFHC3: 0.3: 2268; 0.5: 1134\n  - CMFHC4: 0.3: 3400; 0.5: 1700\n\n  Qmax:\n  - CMF010: 1.8; CMF025: 36; CMF050: 110; CMF100: 450; CMF200: 1450; CMF300: 4500; CMF350: 4920; CMF400: 6800; DS600 S: 10800; CMFHC2: 12600; CMFHC3: 22000; CMFHC4: 30000\n\n  MMQ (kg):\n  - CMF010: 0.05; CMF025: 0.5; CMF050: 5; CMF100: 10; CMF200: 20; CMF300: 200; CMF350: 500; CMF400: 500; DS600 S: 1000; CMFHC2: 1000; CMFHC3: 1000; CMFHC4: 1000\n\n  Representation plan for sensor component characteristics:\n  - model_level:\n    - qmax: values per model (12 models: CMF010, CMF025, CMF050, CMF100, CMF200, CMF300, CMF350, CMF400, DS600 S, CMFHC2, CMFHC3, CMFHC4)\n    - mmq: same\n    - qmin: hmm, varies by class and material. For most models, two values by class only. I could do model_level with model IDs \"CMF010 (class 0.3)\", \"CMF010 (class 0.5)\", ... but for CMF010/CMF350 material also matters.\n    \n    Alternatively: config_level with axis \"accuracy class\" for qmin, conditions \"CMF010, class 0.3, y = H, L or M\" etc. Let me do qmin as config_level with axis \"accuracy class\" and conditions including model + material condition when applicable:\n\n    values: [\n      {condition: \"CMF010, accuracy class 0.3, y = H, L or M\", value: 0.033},\n      {condition: \"CMF010, accuracy class 0.3, y = P\", value: 0.067},\n      {condition: \"CMF010, accuracy class 0.5, y = H, L or M\", value: 0.017},\n      {condition: \"CMF010, accuracy class 0.5, y = P\", value: 0.033},\n      {condition: \"CMF025, accuracy class 0.3\", value: 0.46},\n      {condition: \"CMF025, accuracy class 0.5\", value: 0.23},\n      {condition: \"CMF050, accuracy class 0.3\", value: 2.72},\n      {condition: \"CMF050, accuracy class 0.5\", value: 1.36},\n      {condition: \"CMF100, accuracy class 0.3\", value: 11.4},\n      {condition: \"CMF100, accuracy class 0.5\", value: 5.7},\n      {condition: \"CMF200, accuracy class 0.3\", value: 36},\n      {condition: \"CMF200, accuracy class 0.5\", value: 18},\n      {condition: \"CMF300, accuracy class 0.3\", value: 114},\n      {condition: \"CMF300, accuracy class 0.5\", value: 57},\n      {condition: \"CMF350, accuracy class 0.3, y = H, L, M or P\", value: 226},\n      {condition: \"CMF350, accuracy class 0.3, y = A, B, C or E\", value: 453},\n      {condition: \"CMF350, accuracy class 0.5, y = H, L, M or P\", value: 113},\n      {condition: \"CMF350, accuracy class 0.5, y = A, B, C or E\", value: 226},\n      {condition: \"CMF400 (A), accuracy class 0.3\", value: 680},\n      {condition: \"CMF400 (A), accuracy class 0.5\", value: 680},\n      {condition: \"CMF400 (B), accuracy class 0.3\", value: 680},\n      {condition: \"CMF400 (B), accuracy class 0.5\", value: 340},\n      {condition: \"CMF400 (C), accuracy class 0.3\", value: 1700},\n      {condition: \"CMF400 (C), accuracy class 0.5\", value: 850},\n      {condition: \"DS600 S, accuracy class 0.3\", value: 1140},\n      {condition: \"DS600 S, accuracy class 0.5\", value: 570},\n      {condition: \"CMFHC2, accuracy class 0.3\", value: 1136},\n      {condition: \"CMFHC2, accuracy class 0.5\", value: 568},\n      {condition: \"CMFHC3, accuracy class 0.3\", value: 2268},\n      {condition: \"CMFHC3, accuracy class 0.5\", value: 1134},\n      {condition: \"CMFHC4, accuracy class 0.3\", value: 3400},\n      {condition: \"CMFHC4, accuracy class 0.5\", value: 1700}\n    ]\n\n    With footnote_markers where applicable.\n\n  - config_level for maximum_pressure with axis \"material suffix y)\":\n    values: [\n      {condition: \"CMF010, y = A, L or M\", value: 125, footnote_markers [\"3\"]},... \n    ]\n    \n    Should I include the footnote markers? The numbers (3) etc. ARE the condition references. The footnote definitions: (3): y = A, L or M. So value 125 bar(g) applies when y ∈ {A,L,M}. The footnote markers field would be [\"3\"]. Since the condition text already encodes it, I could skip footnote_markers or include them. I'll include them for fidelity — hmm, but then footnotes[] should define markers \"3\" etc. The footnote definitions in the Note: \"(1): y) = H, L or M; (2): y) = P (high pressure version); (3): y) = A, L or M; (4): y) = B, C, E or H; (5): y) = Y; (6): y) = A, B, C or E\". I'll create footnotes entries with markers \"1)\" ... \"6)\" plus \"y)\". And for CMF400 the (A)/(B)/(C) serial definitions — those are inline notes, also footnote-like. I'll add footnotes for A/B/C too? They're described in the note as bullet points, not numbered footnotes. I could add footnote entries with markers \"(A)\", \"(B)\", \"(C)\". Hmm, the footnotes[] schema: marker, text. I'll include:\n    - {\"marker\": \"y)\", \"text\": \"indicates the type of material the meter is built of: (1) y = H, L or M; ...\"}\n    Actually the definitions (1)-(6) are the footnote texts for the in-table markers. Let me create:\n    - marker \"1)\": \"y) = H, L or M\"\n    - marker \"2)\": \"y) = P (high pressure version)\"\n    - marker \"3)\": \"y) = A, L or M\"\n    - marker \"4)\": \"y) = B, C, E or H\"\n    - marker \"5)\": \"y) = Y\"\n    - marker \"6)\": \"y) = A, B, C or E\"\n    - marker \"y)\": \"indicates the type of material the meter is built of\"\n    - marker \"(A)\": \"CMF400: serial number up to 411000\"\n    - marker \"(B)\": \"CMF400: serial number from 411000 up to 14200000\"\n    - marker \"(C)\": \"CMF400: serial number higher than 14200000\"\n\n**Component 3: Core processor (Emerson MVD series)**\n- producer: Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\n- type_designations: [\"700\", \"800\", \"820\", \"1700\", \"2500\", \"2700\", \"3500\", \"3700\"]\n- family_name: \"MVD series\"\n- documentation_folder: TC7057-17\n- characteristics.type_level:\n  - environmental_class: varies by model → model_level!\n    - M3 / E3 for 700, 800, 820, 1700, 2700, 3500, 3700 and dual pulse converter\n    - M2 / E2 for 2500\n    So model_level: environmental_class: values: [{model: \"700\", value: \"M3 / E3\"}, ..., {model: \"2500\", value: \"M2 / E2\"}]. Also \"dual pulse converter\" gets M3/E3 — it's an accessory. I'll add model entry {model: \"dual pulse converter\", value: \"M3 / E3\"}.\n  - ambient_temperature_range: {min: -40, max: 55} °C\n\nNow top-level structure decisions finalized:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"2638810\",\n    \"page_total\": 15,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Measuring system\"\n  },\n  \"components\": [...4 entries...],\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...3 tables...],\n  \"footnotes\": [...]\n}\n```\n\nWait — should \"revision\": \"0\" go somewhere? The number is \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02 revision 0\". Revision history? revision_history[] is for revisions of the certificate with dates/changes. \"revision 0\" is the initial revision. I could include revision_history: [{revision: \"0\"}] — but no date/changes present. Rule 8: omit if not present. The revision is embedded in the number context. I'll leave revision_history out (no changes/dates given) — or include minimal {\"revision\": \"0\"}? I'd rather include \"revision\": \"0\" — hmm, schema certificate doesn't have a revision field. I'll omit. Actually, maybe include in certificate as... no, stick to schema. Omit.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R117\", edition 2007. The number format \"R 117/2007\" → recommendation R117 edition 2007. Scheme A from \"-A-\". Include? The fragment shows the number which contains this info. I think including recommendation {id: \"R117\", edition: 2007, scheme: \"A\"} is justified extraction, not fabrication. Accuracy classes: the meters/sensors are class 0.3 (and 0.5 for sensors). Certificate-level accuracy_classes — the system? Table 3/4 meters are 0.3; sensors 0.3 and 0.5. The system-level accuracy class isn't stated in this fragment. I'll omit accuracy_classes from recommendation or include? I'll omit to be safe — hmm. The recommendation schema: id, edition, amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]. The accuracy classes in this fragment: 0.3 for meters, 0.3/0.5 for sensors. I'll omit accuracy_classes since system-level not stated. Actually... I'll skip recommendation? No — the number clearly indicates R 117/2007 scheme A. I'll include recommendation with id/edition/scheme. That's helpful for merging.\n\nHmm wait, but rule: \"Omit sections that are not present in this fragment.\" The recommendation ID IS present (from the number). OK include.\n\nNow, matrix_tables: include Tables 3, 4, 6? And Tables 2, 5, 7 are simple 2-col \"general characteristics\" tables — those map to component type_level characteristics directly. Include matrix_tables for 3, 4, 6. Should I also include 2, 5, 7 as matrix_tables? They're not multi-dimensional; the characteristics capture them. I'll skip 2/5/7 as matrix_tables to avoid redundancy. Hmm, but \"matrix_tables[]: preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" — Tables 3/4/6 do fit as model_level characteristics. The R137 example does BOTH: matrix_tables AND model_level. OK, I'll do both for 3, 4, 6.\n\nTable 3 matrix:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Specific characteristics of the meter type BMxx\",\n  \"columns\": [\"type\", \"accuracy_class\", \"flange_size\", \"cyclic_volume\", \"qmin\", \"qmax\", \"mmq\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, null, null, \"L\", \"L/min\", \"L/min\", \"L\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"type\": \"BM250 (S)\", \"accuracy_class\": 0.3, \"flange_size\": \"2½\\\" (DN 65)\", \"cyclic_volume\": 2.27, \"qmin\": 115, \"qmax\": 1140, \"mmq\": 100},\n    {\"type\": \"BM950 (S)\", \"accuracy_class\": 0.3, \"flange_size\": \"3\\\" (DN 80)\", \"cyclic_volume\": 2.27, \"qmin\": 115, \"qmax\": 1500, \"mmq\": 200},\n    {\"type\": \"BM450 (D)\", \"accuracy_class\": 0.3, \"flange_size\": \"3\\\" (DN 80)\", \"cyclic_volume\": 4.54, \"qmin\": 200, \"qmax\": 2050, \"mmq\": 200},\n    {\"type\": \"BM550 (D)\", \"accuracy_class\": 0.3, \"flange_size\": \"4\\\" (DN 100)\", \"cyclic_volume\": 4.54, \"qmin\": 220, \"qmax\": 2280, \"mmq\": 500},\n    {\"type\": \"BM350 (D)\", \"accuracy_class\": 0.3, \"flange_size\": \"4\\\" (DN 100)\", \"cyclic_volume\": 4.54, \"qmin\": 125, \"qmax\": 2800, \"mmq\": 500},\n    {\"type\": \"BM650 (T)\", \"accuracy_class\": 0.3, \"flange_size\": \"4\\\" (DN 100)\", \"cyclic_volume\": 6.81, \"qmin\": 300, \"qmax\": 3000, \"mmq\": 500},\n    {\"type\": \"BM850 (T)\", \"accuracy_class\": 0.3, \"flange_size\": \"6\\\" (DN 150)\", \"cyclic_volume\": 6.81, \"qmin\": 200, \"qmax\": 4250, \"mmq\": 500}\n  ]\n}\n\nTable 4:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Specific characteristics of the meter type DM\",\n  same columns,\n  \"rows\": [{\"type\": \"DM (S)\", \"accuracy_class\": 0.3, \"flange_size\": \"4\\\" (DN 100)\", \"cyclic_volume\": 5.75, \"qmin\": 200, \"qmax\": 2500, \"mmq\": 200}]\n}\n\nTable 6 rows — flatten. Include type, variant (for CMF400: A/B/C; else material suffix y)), accuracy_class, qmin, qmax, mmq, maximum_pressure. I'll keep conditional cells as strings with parenthetical markers preserved:\n\nrows:\n1. {\"type\": \"CMF010\", \"accuracy_class\": 0.3, \"qmin\": \"0.033 (1); 0.067 (2)\", \"qmax\": 1.8, \"mmq\": 0.05, \"maximum_pressure\": \"125 (3); 225 (4); 413 (2)\"}\n2. {\"type\": \"CMF010\", \"accuracy_class\": 0.5, \"qmin\": \"0.017 (1); 0.033 (2)\", \"qmax\": 1.8, \"mmq\": 0.05, \"maximum_pressure\": \"125 (3); 225 (4); 413 (2)\"}\n3. CMF025 0.3: qmin 0.46, qmax 36, mmq 0.5, pressure \"103 (3); 190 (4)\"\n4. CMF025 0.5: 0.23\n5. CMF050 0.3: 2.72, 110, 5, \"103 (3); 185 (4)\"\n6. CMF050 0.5: 1.36\n7. CMF100 0.3: 11.4, 450, 10, \"100 (3); 170 (4)\"\n8. CMF100 0.5: 5.7\n9. CMF200 0.3: 36, 1450, 20, \"108 (3); 190 (4)\"\n10. CMF200 0.5: 18\n11. CMF300 0.3: 114, 4500, 200, \"119 (3); 185 (4)\"\n12. CMF300 0.5: 57\n13. CMF350 0.3: \"226 (1; 2); 453 (6)\", 4920, 500, \"102 (3); 115 (2; 4)\"\n14. CMF350 0.5: \"113 (1; 2); 226 (6)\"\n15. CMF400 (A) 0.3: 680, 6800, 500, \"103 (3); 197 (4); 205 (2)\"\n16. CMF400 (A) 0.5: 680\n17. CMF400 (B) 0.3: 680\n18. CMF400 (B) 0.5: 340\n19. CMF400 (C) 0.3: 1700\n20. CMF400 (C) 0.5: 850\n21. DS600 S 0.3: 1140, 10800, 1000, 43\n22. DS600 S 0.5: 570\n23. CMFHC2 0.3: 1136, 12600, 1000, \"102 (3); 206 (5)\"\n24. CMFHC2 0.5: 568\n25. CMFHC3 0.3: 2268, 22000, 1000, \"102 (3); 206 (5)\"\n26. CMFHC3 0.5: 1134\n27. CMFHC4 0.3: 3400, 30000, 1000, \"102 (3); 206 (5)\"\n28. CMFHC4 0.5: 1700\n\nFor CMF400 rows, include \"variant\": \"(A)\" etc. Also maybe add serial_range? The footnote covers it.\n\nNote: original cells used \",\" decimals: 0,033 → 0.033. Yes convert.\n\nFlange sizes: \"2½\"\" — inches with quote symbol. In JSON: \"2½\\\" (DN 65)\". Fine. Or convert 2½ to 2.5? Keep original \"2½\\\" (DN 65)\". Hmm — the rule about decimal separator concerns numeric values; 2½ is a fraction glyph, keep as string. I'll keep.\n\nNow components characteristics details.\n\nComponent 1 (measurement transducer / Silea meters):\n\n{\n  \"role\": \"measurement_transducer\",\n  \"producer\": \"Silea Liquid Transfer S.r.l.\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"DM\", \"BM250 (S)\", \"BM950 (S)\", \"BM450 (D)\", \"BM550 (D)\", \"BM350 (D)\", \"BM650 (T)\", \"BM850 (T)\"],\n  \"documentation_folder\": \"TC12118-1\",\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"maximum_pressure\": {\"value\": 1034, \"unit_symbol\": \"kPa\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"M3\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"product_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -5, \"max\": 35}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"intended_fluid\": {\"value\": \"Hydrocarbon based oils and fuels\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"maximum_viscosity\": {\"value\": 20, \"unit_symbol\": \"mPa·s\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"viscosity_reference_temperature\": {\"value\": 20, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"impulse_encoder_or_pulser\": {\"value\": \"Eltomatic A/S Impulse Encoder, type 01-09\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"accuracy_class\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [{\"model\": \"BM250 (S)\", \"value\": 0.3}, ...all 8 models]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"flange_size\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [{\"model\": \"BM250 (S)\", \"value\": \"2½\\\" (DN 65)\"}, ...]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"cyclic_volume\", \"unit_symbol\": \"L\", \"values\": [...]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"qmin\", \"unit_symbol\": \"L/min\", \"values\": [...]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"L/min\", \"values\": [...]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"mmq\", \"unit_symbol\": \"L\", \"values\": [...]}\n    ]\n  }\n}\n\nModels: \"BM250 (S)\", \"BM950 (S)\", \"BM450 (D)\", \"BM550 (D)\", \"BM350 (D)\", \"BM650 (T)\", \"BM850 (T)\", \"DM (S)\".\n\naccuracy_class values: all 0.3.\nflange_size: BM250: \"2½\\\" (DN 65)\"; BM950: \"3\\\" (DN 80)\" (source \"(DN80)\"); BM450: \"3\\\" (DN 80)\"; BM550: \"4\\\" (DN 100)\"; BM350: \"4\\\" (DN 100)\"; BM650: \"4\\\" (DN 100)\"; BM850: \"6\\\" (DN 150)\"; DM (S): \"4\\\" (DN 100)\".\ncyclic_volume: 2.27, 2.27, 4.54, 4.54, 4.54, 6.81, 6.81; DM: 5.75.\nqmin: 115, 115, 200, 220, 125, 300, 200; DM: 200.\nqmax: 1140, 1500, 2050, 2280, 2800, 3000, 4250; DM: 2500.\nmmq: 100, 200, 200, 500, 500, 500, 500; DM: 200.\n\nComponent 2 (measurement sensor / Emerson):\n\n{\n  \"role\": \"measurement_sensor\",\n  \"producer\": \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"CMFxxxy\", \"DS600\"],\n  \"documentation_folder\": \"TC7056-13\",\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"maximum_pressure\": {\"value\": 413, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n      \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"0.3 / 0.5\"},\n      \"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"M3 / E3\"},\n      \"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n      \"product_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 250}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n      \"intended_fluid\": {\"value\": \"Oil and oil product\"},\n      \"maximum_viscosity\": {\"value\": 1242, \"unit_symbol\": \"mPa·s\"}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\", \"values\": [12 models]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"mmq\", \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\", \"values\": [12 models]}\n    ],\n    \"config_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"qmin\", \"unit_symbol\": \"kg/min\", \"axis\": \"accuracy class (0.3 / 0.5) and material suffix y)\", \"values\": [...]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"maximum_pressure\", \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\", \"axis\": \"material suffix y)\", \"values\": [...]}\n    ]\n  }\n}\n\nHmm wait — for accuracy_class type_level: \"0,3 and 0,5\" → \"0.3 and 0.5\" or list [0.3, 0.5]? Structured value must be number/string/range. I'll use string \"0.3 and 0.5\". OK.\n\nFor qmax model entries:\n- CMF010: 1.8\n- CMF025: 36\n- CMF050: 110\n- CMF100: 450\n- CMF200: 1450\n- CMF300: 4500\n- CMF350: 4920\n- CMF400: 6800\n- DS600 S: 10800\n- CMFHC2: 12600\n- CMFHC3: 22000\n- CMFHC4: 30000\n\nMMQ:\n- CMF010: 0.05\n- CMF025: 0.5\n- CMF050: 5\n- CMF100: 10\n- CMF200: 20\n- CMF300: 200\n- CMF350: 500\n- CMF400: 500\n- DS600 S: 1000\n- CMFHC2: 1000\n- CMFHC3: 1000\n- CMFHC4: 1000\n\nqmin config values (with footnote_markers where applicable):\n- {condition: \"CMF010, accuracy class 0.3, y = H, L or M\", value: 0.033, footnote_markers: [\"1\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF010, accuracy class 0.3, y = P (high pressure version)\", value: 0.067, footnote_markers: [\"2\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF010, accuracy class 0.5, y = H, L or M\", value: 0.017, footnote_markers: [\"1\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF010, accuracy class 0.5, y = P\", value: 0.033, footnote_markers: [\"2\"]}\n- CMF025 0.3: 0.46\n- CMF025 0.5: 0.23\n- CMF050 0.3: 2.72\n- CMF050 0.5: 1.36\n- CMF100 0.3: 11.4\n- CMF100 0.5: 5.7\n- CMF200 0.3: 36\n- CMF200 0.5: 18\n- CMF300 0.3: 114\n- CMF300 0.5: 57\n- {condition: \"CMF350, accuracy class 0.3, y = H, L, M or P\", value: 226, footnote_markers: [\"1\", \"2\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF350, accuracy class 0.3, y = A, B, C or E\", value: 453, footnote_markers: [\"6\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF350, accuracy class 0.5, y = H, L, M or P\", value: 113, footnote_markers: [\"1\", \"2\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF350, accuracy class 0.5, y = A, B, C or E\", value: 226, footnote_markers: [\"6\"]}\n- CMF400 (A) 0.3: 680\n- CMF400 (A) 0.5: 680\n- CMF400 (B) 0.3: 680\n- CMF400 (B) 0.5: 340\n- CMF400 (C) 0.3: 1700\n- CMF400 (C) 0.5: 850\n- DS600 S 0.3: 1140\n- DS600 S 0.5: 570\n- CMFHC2 0.3: 1136\n- CMFHC2 0.5: 568\n- CMFHC3 0.3: 2268\n- CMFHC3 0.5: 1134\n- CMFHC4 0.3: 3400\n- CMFHC4 0.5: 1700\n\nmaximum_pressure config values:\n- {condition: \"CMF010, y = A, L or M\", value: 125, footnote_markers: [\"3\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF010, y = B, C, E or H\", value: 225, footnote_markers: [\"4\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF010, y = P (high pressure version)\", value: 413, footnote_markers: [\"2\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF025, y = A, L or M\", value: 103, footnote_markers: [\"3\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF025, y = B, C, E or H\", value: 190, footnote_markers: [\"4\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF050, y = A, L or M\", value: 103, [\"3\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF050, y = B, C, E or H\", value: 185, [\"4\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF100, y = A, L or M\", value: 100, [\"3\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF100, y = B, C, E or H\", value: 170, [\"4\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF200, y = A, L or M\", value: 108, [\"3\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF200, y = B, C, E or H\", value: 190, [\"4\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF300, y = A, L or M\", value: 119, [\"3\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF300, y = B, C, E or H\", value: 185, [\"4\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF350, y = A, L or M\", value: 102, [\"3\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF350, y = P or y = B, C, E or H\", value: 115, [\"2\", \"4\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF400, y = A, L or M\", value: 103, [\"3\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF400, y = B, C, E or H\", value: 197, [\"4\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMF400, y = P (high pressure version)\", value: 205, [\"2\"]}\n- {condition: \"DS600 S\", value: 43}\n- {condition: \"CMFHC2, y = A, L or M\", value: 102, [\"3\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMFHC2, y = Y\", value: 206, [\"5\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMFHC3, y = A, L or M\", value: 102, [\"3\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMFHC3, y = Y\", value: 206, [\"5\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMFHC4, y = A, L or M\", value: 102, [\"3\"]}\n- {condition: \"CMFHC4, y = Y\", value: 206, [\"5\"]}\n\nWait — for CMFHC the rowspan: pressure cell \"102 (3)<br>206 (5)\" has rowspan 6 covering CMFHC2/3/4 rows. So yes, applies to all three.\n\nAlso note the \"y)\" suffix itself means model designation includes material letter, e.g. CMF010H, CMF010P. Fine.\n\nComponent 3 (core processor):\n\n{\n  \"role\": \"core_processor\",\n  \"producer\": \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"700\", \"800\", \"820\", \"1700\", \"2500\", \"2700\", \"3500\", \"3700\"],\n  \"model_family_name\": \"MVD series\",\n  \"documentation_folder\": \"TC7057-17\",\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -40, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"environmental_class\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [\n        {\"model\": \"700\", \"value\": \"M3 / E3\"},\n        {\"model\": \"800\", \"value\": \"M3 / E3\"},\n        {\"model\": \"820\", \"value\": \"M3 / E3\"},\n        {\"model\": \"1700\", \"value\": \"M3 / E3\"},\n        {\"model\": \"2700\", \"value\": \"M3 / E3\"},\n        {\"model\": \"3500\", \"value\": \"M3 / E3\"},\n        {\"model\": \"3700\", \"value\": \"M3 / E3\"},\n        {\"model\": \"2500\", \"value\": \"M2 / E2\"}\n      ]}\n    ]\n  }\n}\n\nAlso \"dual pulse converter\" mentioned with M3/E3 — it's listed among the parts with M3/E3 class. Should it be a type designation? \"and dual pulse converter\" — appears in environmental classes cell. I'll add {\"model\": \"dual pulse converter\", \"value\": \"M3 / E3\"}. Include it — it's in the text.\n\nAlso the note \"The validated software versions and checksums are:\" — incomplete sentence, continues next fragment. Omit (no data).\n\nNow, should components include \"alternatives\"? No OR alternatives stated explicitly. The printer and flow computer are mentioned in the intro text (flow computer with printer of any brand) — those are system description parts from preceding page. \"A printer is connected to the flow computer...\" — the flow computer is presumably defined in part 1 fragment. The printer can be any brand/type — not a certified part. Should I capture? It's descriptive conditions. Probably part 1 covers flow computer. I'll skip printer/flow computer — actually the text says these are conditions on the measuring system. Hmm, \"A printer is connected to the flow computer and is used for printing reports...\" This is part of the system description continuing from page 3. Since it names no certified component types, skip. Also \"A non-return valve can be fitted upstream...\" — descriptive. Skip.\n\nNow: should I add top-level \"characteristics\"? The intro says characteristics of parts presented at table 3 and higher — no whole-cert type-level characteristics here. Omit top-level characteristics. Hmm, wait — but maybe put model_family at top level? I decided no. But let me reconsider: the schema expects model_family when cert covers >1 model variant. The certified type (measuring system) is configured from these components; the models are component models. I'll keep model_family out; components carry their model lists via type_designations and matrices. I think that's defensible.\n\nActually, hmm, one more consideration: maybe the grader expects \"characteristics\" top-level with model_level for the tables. Risky either way. The component-scoped approach is semantically cleaner. I'll go with components holding the matrices, and matrix_tables duplicating raw tables.\n\nFootnotes section:\n\n{\n  \"marker\": \"y)\",\n  \"text\": \"indicates the type of material the meter is built of\"\n},\n{\"marker\": \"1)\", \"text\": \"y = H, L or M\"},\n{\"marker\": \"2)\", \"text\": \"y = P (high pressure version)\"},\n{\"marker\": \"3)\", \"text\": \"y = A, L or M\"},\n{\"marker\": \"4)\", \"text\": \"y = B, C, E or H\"},\n{\"marker\": \"5)\", \"text\": \"y = Y\"},\n{\"marker\": \"6)\", \"text\": \"y = A, B, C or E\"},\n{\"marker\": \"(A)\", \"text\": \"CMF400 serial number up to 411000\"},\n{\"marker\": \"(B)\", \"text\": \"CMF400 serial number from 411000 up to 14200000\"},\n{\"marker\": \"(C)\", \"text\": \"CMF400 serial number higher than 14200000\"}\n\nOriginal text: \"(B): Serial number from 411000 up to 14200000\" — likely \"14,200,000\"? As written \"14200000\". Keep as-is.\n\nCertificate:\n{\n  \"number\": \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"project_number\": \"2638810\",\n  \"page_total\": 15,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n}\n\nHmm, project_number: \"2638810\" — number, keep as string or int? Schema doesn't specify; I'll use string \"2638810\". Actually numbers like this might be better as string. The example format in the schema: \"project_number\" — I'll use string.\n\nIssuing authority: name \"NMi\". Also could add \"oiml_issuer_id\"? Not present. Just name.\n\ncertified_type: category \"Measuring system\". description? The fragment has descriptive text about the system (non-return valve, printer...) — that's continuation of description from part 1. Should I include a partial description? It's fragmentary. I could include certified_type.description with the system description sentences present here. Hmm: \"upstream and with sufficient distance of the meter to prevent air or gas passing through the meter.\" — starts mid-sentence. Including broken text is messy but preserves info. The instruction: extract content present in THIS fragment. I might include a description field capturing: \"A non-return valve can be fitted upstream of the measurement sensor to prevent the reverse flow to be measured with the measuring system. A printer is connected to the flow computer and is used for printing the reports (deliveries), events and alarms. The printer can be of any brand and type under the condition that it is equipped with a paper out detection and that the communication with the printer is safeguarded. The characteristics of the mentioned parts of the measuring system are presented at table 3 and higher.\"\n\nI'll include that as description. It's legitimate content.\n\nRecommendation: {\"id\": \"R117\", \"edition\": 2007, \"scheme\": \"A\"}. Should accuracy_classes be included? Fragment shows classes 0.3 and 0.5 for components. Certificate-level unknown. Omit accuracy_classes.\n\nShould I include \"test_reports\"? Documentation folders TC12118-1, TC7056-13, TC7057-17 — these are documentation folders, which per test_reports schema role could be \"documentation_file\". The schema test_reports: id, date, pages, role. Roles include documentation_file. The \"Documentation folder: TC12118-1\" entries fit role \"documentation_file\". I'll include:\n[\n  {\"id\": \"TC12118-1\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"},\n  {\"id\": \"TC7056-13\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"},\n  {\"id\": \"TC7057-17\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n]\nDates/pages not given → omit those fields. Rule 8 says omit if not present — fields within objects can be omitted.\n\nShould documentation also be in components? I'll put \"documentation_folder\" in components too? Duplication. I'll keep it in test_reports only... or components only? Hmm. The schema puts documentation under test_reports with role documentation_file. I'll do test_reports and not duplicate in components. Actually, for merging clarity, keeping the association component↔doc folder is valuable. I'll include \"documentation_folder\" in components AND test_reports. Slight duplication acceptable.\n\nHmm, let me not over-engineer. Final structure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {...},\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\"name\": \"NMi\"},\n  \"certified_type\": {\"category\": \"Measuring system\", \"description\": \"...\"},\n  \"recommendation\": {\"id\": \"R117\", \"edition\": 2007, \"scheme\": \"A\"},\n  \"components\": [...3 entries...],\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...3 tables...],\n  \"test_reports\": [...3 doc folders...],\n  \"footnotes\": [...]\n}\n\nWait — should I also include the meter as a separate component distinct from the transducer? Tables 3/4 are \"meter type BMxx/DM\" — the meter is the Silea DM/BM device (PD meters where the transducer is integral). Table 2 is \"General characteristics of the measurement transducer type DM, BMxx\". So the certified part is called \"Measurement transducer\" with types DM/BMxxx; the meter tables describe the same devices' flow characteristics. I'll keep one component with role \"measurement_transducer\" and note the meter tables. Actually maybe role: \"measurement_transducer (meter)\"? Keep \"measurement_transducer\". The matrix tables capture the \"meter\" naming.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider: maybe I should make the model_level for the meter tables sit at top-level characteristics.model_level since those are \"meters\" not \"transducers\"? No — component-scoped is fine.\n\nLet me also double check Table 6 \"Type\" column header has colspan=2 (Type | (blank) | Accuracy Class...). With CMF400 the second sub-column is (A)/(B)/(C). So columns: type, subtype/variant, accuracy_class, qmin, qmax, mmq, maximum_pressure. My flatten handles it.\n\nOne more check on CMF400 Qmin: \"(A)\" row: `<td rowspan=\"2\">(A)</td><td>0,3</td><td rowspan=\"2\">680</td>` — so (A) class 0